WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.267
This table shows why storage alone is not enough.

00:00:03.267 --> 00:00:07.892
ZIP codes may look numeric, but arithmetic on them is meaningless.

00:00:07.892 --> 00:00:12.707
Satisfaction ratings use numbers but represent ordered categories.

00:00:12.707 --> 00:00:15.317
Emergency visits are true counts.

00:00:15.317 --> 00:00:20.747
Celsius temperature is a numeric measurement, but its zero point is arbitrary.

00:00:20.747 --> 00:00:26.432
When you see a numeric-looking column, first ask what the numbers mean in the real world.

00:00:26.432 --> 00:00:31.462
Only after that should you choose summaries, encodings, or models.

00:00:31.462 --> 00:00:38.742
Connect this idea to a university analyzing student records, surveys, and learning-platform activity.

00:00:38.742 --> 00:00:44.477
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:52.287
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:52.287 --> 00:00:58.227
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:58.227 --> 00:01:03.894
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
